From: Grische <opensource@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 20:21:10 +0200
Subject: wifi: ath10k: store the HTT tx queue state map as little endian

The host exposes its queue state to the firmware through struct
htt_q_state, a buffer the firmware fetches by DMA in push-pull mode.
Every field in it is either a byte or __le32, except the per TID peer
bitmap, which is a native u32 updated with plain bit operations. On a
big-endian host the firmware therefore sees each map word byte-swapped
and looks up the wrong peers when it decides which host queues to fetch.

Within each 32-bit word host peer n lands on firmware bit
(3 - n % 32 / 8) * 8 + n % 8, so peers 0-7 swap with 24-31 and 8-15
with 16-23. Once the firmware switches a radio to push-pull, a station
is only fetched while its swap partner has frames queued. On a QCA9984
in a big-endian MIPS router this stalled unicast to most clients and
mesh peers on the affected radio, while broadcast and multicast kept
working through the vdev peers. Clients still associated and got a DHCP
lease, but reached nothing.

Declare the map as __le32 and convert the bit with cpu_to_le32() before
setting or clearing it.

Tested-on: QCA9984 hw1.0 PCI 10.4-3.9.0.2-00157

Fixes: c1a43d9720d8 ("ath10k: implement updating shared htt txq state")
Assisted-by: LLM sparse
Signed-off-by: Grische <opensource@grische.xyz>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt.h    | 5 +++--
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt_tx.c | 4 ++--
 2 files changed, 5 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt.h
@@ -1540,7 +1540,8 @@ struct htt_frag_desc_bank_cfg64 {
  *	HTT_Q_DEPTH_TYPE_MSDUS the number of packets is scaled by 2 **
  *	record_multiplier (see htt_q_state_conf).
  * @map: Used by firmware to quickly check which host queues are not empty. It
- *	is a bitmap simply saying.
+ *	is a bitmap simply saying. The firmware reads each word as little
+ *	endian, so peer n is bit n % 32 of word n / 32 in that byte order.
  * @seq: Used by firmware to quickly check if the host queues were updated
  *	since it last checked.
  *
@@ -1548,7 +1549,7 @@ struct htt_frag_desc_bank_cfg64 {
  */
 struct htt_q_state {
 	u8 count[HTT_TX_Q_STATE_NUM_TIDS][HTT_TX_Q_STATE_NUM_PEERS];
-	u32 map[HTT_TX_Q_STATE_NUM_TIDS][(HTT_TX_Q_STATE_NUM_PEERS + 31) / 32];
+	__le32 map[HTT_TX_Q_STATE_NUM_TIDS][(HTT_TX_Q_STATE_NUM_PEERS + 31) / 32];
 	__le32 seq;
 } __packed;
 
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt_tx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt_tx.c
@@ -79,8 +79,8 @@ static void __ath10k_htt_tx_txq_recalc(s
 	}
 
 	ar->htt.tx_q_state.vaddr->count[tid][peer_id] = count;
-	ar->htt.tx_q_state.vaddr->map[tid][idx] &= ~bit;
-	ar->htt.tx_q_state.vaddr->map[tid][idx] |= count ? bit : 0;
+	ar->htt.tx_q_state.vaddr->map[tid][idx] &= ~cpu_to_le32(bit);
+	ar->htt.tx_q_state.vaddr->map[tid][idx] |= count ? cpu_to_le32(bit) : 0;
 
 	ath10k_dbg(ar, ATH10K_DBG_HTT, "htt tx txq state update peer_id %u tid %u count %u\n",
 		   peer_id, tid, count);
